encyclopedia :crowncut_shifts do
  name "Crowncut Shifts"
  summary "Crowncut Shifts are refinery work-families who keep contaminated clothing outside their homes and take meals together at the work boundary. The boundary meal is the institution: half wash-line, half family table, held where the work ends and the household begins."
  kind :culture
  subkind :work_tradition
  status :complete
  topics :salvage, :household, :"social-structure", :danger
  prevalence :uncommon
  appears_when all: { place: [:yard] }

  descriptive_identity(
    manner: "Shift families measure closeness in boundary meals shared — 'we eat at their " \
            "line' is the strongest thing one family says of another — and observe the " \
            "clean-side and work-side division of the world with a rigor outsiders " \
            "mistake for superstition until they see the dosimetry.",
    attire: "Work-side dress is communal property that stays at the line, washed and " \
            "reissued by the boundary house; clean-side dress is personal, and crossing " \
            "the line in the wrong clothes is the culture's one unforgivable slovenliness."
  )

  prose <<~PROSE
    The families that work the great salvage refineries — Crowncut's crews first, and the pattern spread with the trade — solved contamination with architecture and made the architecture a culture. The boundary house stands at the line where refinery ground ends: wash rooms, clothing stores, and the long tables where every shift eats. Work-side clothing stays work-side, communal, washed and reissued at the line; bodies cross the boundary scrubbed and re-dressed; and the meal taken at the tables between shift and home is the institution the culture is named for — the place where the work is talked out, the crews mix across families, and the day's grime is left with the day's clothes before either can walk home to the children.

    The boundary meal grew from decontamination protocol into the shift families' whole social architecture. Marriages start at the long tables and are announced there; disputes between crews are settled at them under the custom that what is spoken work-side stays work-side; the elderly retired hold their places and their opinions at the head of each table, dosimetry badges framed on the boundary-house wall behind them like ancestors. The culture's discipline is cheerful and absolute in the way of rules written in old losses: the early refinery generations took the work home in their clothes and buried the results, the boundary custom is the monument, and a shift family teaching a new hire the wash order is performing its founding history whether the hire knows it or not.
  PROSE

  cue "At the refinery line, a long table fills for the boundary meal — scrubbed workers in clean-side dress, the work clothes already hung back in the communal stores — and three families' children run between the benches."
  cue "The boundary-house wall carries framed dosimetry badges going back generations, hung where every meal can see them, in the place other cultures hang portraits."
  affordance "The boundary custom keeps the trade's poison out of the trade's homes with an architecture any refinery can copy — and the boundary meal makes the line where work ends into the community's strongest social institution rather than its loneliest checkpoint."
  pressure "The custom holds exactly as long as every crossing honors it, so the culture polices its line with familial intensity — and the shortcut taken once, in the wrong clothes, with good reasons, is the story every boundary house tells with the founding losses' names attached."
  variation "Crowncut's original tables seat by shift regardless of family; the younger refineries seat by family regardless of shift, and each tradition considers the other's arrangement the obvious social error."
  variation "Retired workers keep their table places for life, and the framed badge wall behind the head seats is read to new hires on their first boundary meal, name by name."
end
